<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Spec section 14: minimum required capabilities only. `packageNames` scopes
  every event this service receives to the two supported driver apps — the
  system never delivers events from any other app to JobFilter, which is the
  first line of defense against interacting with the wrong application.

  canPerformGestures is required because Bolt's job-offer card is
  accepted/rejected by swiping right/left on the card itself, not by
  tapping a button (see JobAccessibilityService.swipeJobCard).

  canTakeScreenshot lets Uber's fare/route text be read via OCR when the
  accessibility tree doesn't expose it (see OcrTextProvider) — without this
  attribute, AccessibilityService.takeScreenshot() throws a
  SecurityException at runtime rather than failing at build time.

  flagIncludeNotImportantViews: added after a real-device finding that this
  service's own tree search for Uber's Confirm/Match button returned only
  empty ViewGroups, while `uiautomator dump` (which runs via UiAutomation, a
  separate, more privileged accessibility connection that includes
  "not-important" views by default) saw the real, labeled Button nodes at
  the exact same moment. By default, AccessibilityServices only receive
  nodes Android considers "important for accessibility" — everything else
  collapses into an empty placeholder in the exposed tree, which matches
  that finding exactly. This flag is the public, documented way to ask for
  the same full view as UiAutomation gets, with no ADB/root/private API
  involved. If it doesn't fully resolve the gap, the diagnostic logging in
  JobAccessibilityService (window type/displayId/bounds, and
  isImportantForAccessibility per node) narrows down what does.

  typeWindowsChanged: added after a real device proved
  typeWindowStateChanged/typeWindowContentChanged alone never fire when
  Uber shows its job-offer overlay while a DIFFERENT app is genuinely in
  the foreground (both are scoped to whichever window Android currently
  considers "active", which isn't Uber's overlay in that case) — no event
  reached this service at all, so the badge never showed. typeWindowsChanged
  instead fires whenever the set of visible windows changes at all (a
  window added/removed/resized), independent of which app is "active" —
  see JobAccessibilityService.onAccessibilityEvent for why it can't trust
  this event's own packageName and re-scans `windows` directly instead.

  notificationTimeout=0 (was 200): this is a deliberate minimum delay Android
  inserts between successive events of the same type from the same source
  before delivering the first one — a real device showed the decision badge
  displaying "slightly late" relative to the job card appearing, and this
  was pure unnecessary wait on top of capture+OCR time. Safe to drop to zero
  because onAccessibilityEvent's own lastEmittedText check already discards
  redundant re-processing of identical content, which is what this timeout
  exists to protect against in the first place.

  typeNotificationStateChanged: Bolt posts a genuine Android heads-up
  notification ("New ride request") the instant an offer arrives — a real
  device confirmed this is a system notification, not part of Bolt's own app
  UI, and it stays visible for a while (the driver dismisses it manually,
  not a short auto-timeout). It's a more immediate and reliable "a job just
  arrived" signal than waiting for Bolt's in-app window content to change,
  so onAccessibilityEvent uses it as an extra trigger to re-scan monitored
  windows right away, on top of the existing typeWindowsChanged/
  typeWindowContentChanged triggers.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeWindowStateChanged|typeWindowContentChanged|typeWindowsChanged|typeNotificationStateChanged"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagReportViewIds|flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows|flagIncludeNotImportantViews"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"
    android:canPerformGestures="true"
    android:canTakeScreenshot="true"
    android:canRequestTouchExplorationMode="false"
    android:notificationTimeout="0"
    android:packageNames="com.ubercab.driver,ee.mtakso.driver"
    android:description="@string/accessibility_service_description"/>
